# Task
Below is the raw scraping result of a web source: a news article, a social media post, an official record or register entry, a database page, a document rendered as text, a video page, or similar. Besides the source's own content, the scrape usually contains page clutter. **Your task is to identify the lines that make up the source's main content.** Each line is prefixed with its line number.

Media are shown as inline references like `<image:N>` or `<video:N>`, where `N` is a number. Treat them as part of the line they stand on.

## What to Keep
- The title or headline
- The author, account name, byline and date line
- The main text: article body, post text, record or table contents, document text, video description or transcript
- The media that belong to the content itself: images, videos and embedded posts that the source presents as part of its content, together with their captions and credits
- For social media posts: the post itself, including its media, even if they are shown above the text

## What to Remove
- Navigation, menus, breadcrumbs and search bars
- Ads and sponsored content
- Cookie, consent and privacy notices
- Newsletter, subscription and app download prompts
- Share, like and follow buttons, and other UI elements
- "Related", "Recommended", "Most read", "Read also" and similar lists of other content, including their thumbnails
- Comments and replies by other users
- Footers, legal notices and copyright lines
- Login, paywall and "sign up to continue" text

